==Russian==

===Etymology 1===
From {{OCS.|ru}} {{term|sc=Cyrl|градъ|tr=gradŭ|lang=cu}}, from {{proto|Slavic|gradъ|lang=ru}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|greh₃d-|lang=ru}}. Cognate with Lithuanian [[grúodas]] (Balto-Slavic *grōda-), Latin {{term|grando|grandō|lang=la}}, Armenian {{term|կարկուտ|lang=hy|tr=karkut}} (from Old Armenian *gagrōdo-), Old Indic *hrādúniṣ, and possibly with English {{term|grind}}.

====Noun====
'''град''' (grad) {{m}}

#{{meteorology|lang=ru}} [[hail]]
#:*Град [[идти|идёт]] — ''It’s hailing.''
#{{meteorology|lang=ru}} [[shower]], [[rain]]
# [[volley]], [[shower]]
#:*град [[пуля|пуль]] — ''hail of bullets''

=====Declension=====
{{ru-noun1|град||гра́да||гра́ду||град||гра́дом||о гра́де|}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{OCS.|ru}} {{term|sc=Cyrl|градъ|tr=gradŭ|lang=cu}}, from {{proto|Slavic|gordъ|settlement, enclosed place|lang=ru}}, from {{proto|Balto-Slavic|gordos|lang=ru}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|gʰordʰ-o-|lang=ru}}, either directly or possibly via Proto-Germanic '''*garda'''. Cognate with English {{term|garden}}, {{term|yard}}, {{term|gird}}, German {{term|Garten|lang=de}}, Danish {{term|gård|lang=da}}, Norwegian {{term|gard|lang=no}}.

====Noun====
'''град''' (grad) {{m}}

# ''(pоetic, antiquated)'': [[town]], [[city]]

====Declension====
